/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Lowering VDD makes gates slower, so it must be paid for in speed.
   - Clock gating stops the clock to registers that have no new data.
   - Power gating puts a large switch between a block and a rail: a pMOS header to VDD or an nMOS footer to ground, usually a high-Vt device that leaks little itself.
   - Multi-threshold (multi-Vt) cells.
   - DVFS picks the lowest voltage and frequency pair that meets the current workload, from a table checked at every process corner.
   - Order matters.
   - A power manager ties these together: the operating system or a hardware monitor reports the workload, and the manager sets the DVFS point, gates clocks and switches power domains.
*/
