/* book: lead: Static CMOS builds every gate from two complementary networks: an nMOS pull-down network (PDN) that connects the output to ground and a pMOS pull-up network (PUN) that connects it to VDD. For every input combination exactly one of them conducts, so the output always sits at a rail and no steady current flows. Ratioed logic keeps the pull-down network but replaces the pull-up with one always-on loa */
/* book Figure 3.9.1: The six-transistor gate Y = NOT(A·(B + C)) of the worked example, with each width chosen for the drive of a unit inverter. The pull-up is the dual of the pull-down: the series pair B, C becomes a parallel pair below the output. */
