/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A simple N-input gate uses 2N transistors.
   - Some syllabi call this style complementary CMOS.
   - In pseudo-nMOS the PUN is a single pMOS with its gate at ground.
   - The load current is (kp/2)(VDD − |Vtp|)² while the pMOS stays saturated, which needs VOL ≤ |Vtp|.
   - A tristate inverter adds an enable nMOS and pMOS in series with the two networks; when disabled both paths are open and the output floats (high impedance), so several drivers can share a bus.
*/
